questions.answer:
async function chunkedMap(items, fn, size = 100) {
  const out = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i += size) {
    const chunk = items.slice(i, i + size);
    out.push(...chunk.map(fn));
    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 0));
  }

  return out;
}

chunkedMap([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], (n) => n * 2, 2).then((r) => console.log(r));
// [ 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 ]

The loop does synchronous work for one chunk, then awaits a timer. That await returns control to the event loop, which can run input handlers and render a frame before the next chunk. Without the yield, the whole array is one long task and the page stays frozen until it finishes. The final await adds one unnecessary tick; guard it if the timing matters. For CPU-heavy per-item work, a Worker is better than chunking because chunking still uses the main thread.
